# Fixing Deep @Input Prop-Drilling With a Shared Signal-Based Service

## 1. What is it?

This is a scenario about **prop drilling**: a top-level component owns some data, a component six levels below it needs to read that data, and the current code passes it down as an `@Input()` through every single level in between — even though levels 2 through 5 don't actually use the data themselves, they just forward it. On top of that, two more components elsewhere in the app (not descendants of that same tree at all) also need to react whenever the data changes — which `@Input()` drilling can't even reach, since `@Input()` only flows downward through a direct ancestor chain.

The fix is the same core idea as any "multiple components need to share state without direct coupling" problem: pull the shared data out of the component tree entirely and into an injectable, signal-based service. Every component that actually needs the data — whether it's 6 levels deep or off in a completely different branch — injects the service directly and reads the signal itself. No intermediate component needs to know the data exists at all.

## 2. Why does this happen (and why is it a problem)?

`@Input()` is designed for **direct, one-hop parent-to-child configuration** — a parent explicitly deciding what a specific child component should render, the same way you'd pass an argument to a function. It works great for that. It was never designed to be a transport mechanism for shuttling state across an entire subtree to some distant descendant.

Once a piece of state needs to cross more than one or two levels, `@Input()` drilling causes real problems:

- **Every intermediate component gets polluted with data it doesn't care about.** Levels 2 through 5 in this scenario have to declare an `@Input()`, accept the value, and manually re-pass it to their own child — purely as plumbing. None of them actually use it.
- **Refactoring becomes fragile.** Renaming the property, changing its shape, or inserting/removing a level in the component tree means touching every intermediate component along the chain, even though the actual producer and actual consumer haven't conceptually changed at all.
- **It fundamentally can't reach sideways.** `@Input()` only flows down a direct ancestor-to-descendant chain. The two other distant components in this scenario aren't descendants of the same subtree — there's no `@Input()` chain that reaches them at all. The only way to make `@Input()`/`@Output()` work here would be bubbling an event all the way up to a common ancestor and then drilling it back down multiple separate chains — multiplying the exact problem you're trying to get rid of.

## 3. How does it work?

- Pull the shared piece of state out of the component tree and into a service, marked `@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })` so it's a single, app-wide singleton instance.
- Store the actual value as a `signal()` inside that service, exposed to the outside world as **read-only** (`.asReadonly()`), with an explicit method for the one place that's allowed to change it (the top-level "owner" component).
- The **top-level component** (the one that used to be the root of the `@Input()` chain) injects the service and calls its update method whenever the underlying data changes — it doesn't pass anything down through its template anymore.
- The **deeply nested component** injects the exact same service directly via `inject()`, and reads the signal itself. It doesn't matter how many levels deep it is — dependency injection resolves the same singleton instance regardless of tree depth, so there's no "hop-by-hop" cost the way there is with `@Input()`.
- **Every intermediate level (2 through 5) goes back to knowing nothing about this data at all** — no `@Input()`, no forwarding, nothing. They're purely structural/layout components again.
- The **two distant components**, wherever they live in the app, inject the same service too. Since DI resolves to the same singleton no matter where a component sits in the tree, "distant" and "deeply nested" are treated identically — there's no special-casing needed for either case, which is a big part of why this approach scales so much better than `@Input()`/`@Output()` chains.
- Because it's a `signal()`, every consumer's template (or `computed()`) reading it re-renders automatically whenever it changes — no manual subscription/unsubscription lifecycle needed anywhere.

## 4. Example

**Before — the problem this scenario describes** (shown briefly, not the fix): every intermediate level has to accept and re-forward an `@Input()` it never actually uses itself:

```ts
// Level2Component, Level3Component, Level4Component, Level5Component all look like this —
// pure plumbing, repeated at every level:
@Component({
  selector: 'app-level2',
  standalone: true,
  template: `<app-level3 [customer]="customer" />`
})
export class Level2Component {
  @Input() customer!: Customer; // never used here, just re-passed down
}
```

And the two distant components can't be reached by this chain at all without a separate, parallel wiring effort.

**After — the fix**, shown in full:

**`selected-customer.service.ts`** — the one place the shared state actually lives:

```ts
import { Injectable, signal } from '@angular/core';

export interface Customer {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  plan: string;
}

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class SelectedCustomerService {
  private _selectedCustomer = signal<Customer | null>(null);
  readonly selectedCustomer = this._selectedCustomer.asReadonly();

  select(customer: Customer): void {
    this._selectedCustomer.set(customer);
  }
}
```

**`dashboard.component.ts`** — the top-level "owner," the only place that writes to the service:

```ts
import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { SelectedCustomerService, Customer } from './selected-customer.service';
import { Level2Component } from './level2.component';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-dashboard',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [Level2Component],
  template: `
    <div class="customer-picker">
      @for (customer of customers; track customer.id) {
        <button type="button" (click)="pick(customer)">{{ customer.name }}</button>
      }
    </div>
    <app-level2 />
  `
})
export class DashboardComponent {
  private selection = inject(SelectedCustomerService);

  customers: Customer[] = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Acme Corp', plan: 'Enterprise' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Globex', plan: 'Pro' },
    { id: 3, name: 'Initech', plan: 'Starter' }
  ];

  pick(customer: Customer): void {
    this.selection.select(customer);
  }
}
```

**Levels 2 through 5** — now completely ignorant of the data, purely structural (this is the entire point — compare to the "before" version above):

```ts
// level2.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { Level3Component } from './level3.component';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-level2',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [Level3Component],
  template: `<div class="level">Level 2 (no @Input, no knowledge of customer data)<app-level3 /></div>`
})
export class Level2Component {}
```

```ts
// level3.component.ts / level4.component.ts / level5.component.ts follow the exact same
// pattern — each just renders the next level down, with zero data-related code.
```

**`level6.component.ts`** — six levels deep, reads the data directly:

```ts
import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { SelectedCustomerService } from './selected-customer.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-level6',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <div class="level deep">
      <p>Level 6 (6 levels deep) — reads the service directly, no @Input chain needed.</p>
      @if (selection.selectedCustomer(); as customer) {
        <p>Showing details for: <b>{{ customer.name }}</b> ({{ customer.plan }})</p>
      } @else {
        <p>No customer selected yet.</p>
      }
    </div>
  `
})
export class Level6Component {
  selection = inject(SelectedCustomerService);
}
```

**Two distant components, elsewhere in the app entirely** — same trick, no special-casing needed:

```ts
// header-badge.component.ts
import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { SelectedCustomerService } from './selected-customer.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-header-badge',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <div class="badge">
      Header badge: {{ selection.selectedCustomer()?.name ?? 'none selected' }}
    </div>
  `
})
export class HeaderBadgeComponent {
  selection = inject(SelectedCustomerService);
}
```

```ts
// sidebar-summary.component.ts
import { Component, inject, computed } from '@angular/core';
import { SelectedCustomerService } from './selected-customer.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-sidebar-summary',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <div class="sidebar">
      Sidebar summary: {{ summary() }}
    </div>
  `
})
export class SidebarSummaryComponent {
  selection = inject(SelectedCustomerService);
  summary = computed(() => {
    const customer = this.selection.selectedCustomer();
    return customer ? `${customer.name} is on the ${customer.plan} plan` : 'No customer selected';
  });
}
```

**`app.component.ts`** — lays out all three independent branches; note it wires nothing between them:

```ts
import { Component, OnInit, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { DashboardComponent } from './dashboard.component';
import { HeaderBadgeComponent } from './header-badge.component';
import { SidebarSummaryComponent } from './sidebar-summary.component';
import { SelectedCustomerService } from './selected-customer.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [DashboardComponent, HeaderBadgeComponent, SidebarSummaryComponent],
  template: `
    <app-header-badge />
    <app-dashboard />
    <app-sidebar-summary />
  `
})
export class AppComponent implements OnInit {
  private selection = inject(SelectedCustomerService);

  ngOnInit(): void {
    // Simulates picking "Acme Corp" on the dashboard, with no click needed to verify.
    this.selection.select({ id: 1, name: 'Acme Corp', plan: 'Enterprise' });
  }
}
```

